{
  "email": "hannah.lee@brightline.example",
  "reason": "KB-008 quick check: see whether similar phishing messages were caught"
}

{
  "summary": "Reported: Hannah Lee (Payroll Specialist, Finance, user_id 12) received an email from \"IT Support\" asking her to reply with her password to finish setting up her account. She says she did NOT reply or share her password. She did not mention clicking links or opening attachments. Checked: account active, 0 failed logins, not blocked; must_change_password=true (password last set 2026-10-09), so she may really be a new or recently reset account. Phishers may be targeting new hires and payroll staff. Quarantine for her mailbox is empty, so this message got past filtering. Not known: sender address, exact subject, whether it came from an internal account, and whether other users got it. User was told to leave the email in her mailbox. Needed: security to pull the message from her mailbox, review the sender and headers, block the sender or domain across the org, and check for other recipients. If the sender is internal, treat that account as possibly compromised.",
  "reason": "KB-008: always escalate reported suspicious emails so security can review and block the sender"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, thanks for reporting this, and well done for not replying. That email is not real. Brightline IT will never ask you to send your password by email or any other way. Please don't reply to it, click any links in it, or forward it to anyone. Just leave it in your mailbox for now so our security team can look at it. I've passed it to them, and they'll check the sender and block it for everyone if needed. If you did click a link, open an attachment, or type your password anywhere, tell us right away so we can protect your account. Also, never approve a Duo prompt you didn't start yourself.",
  "internal_note": "Followed KB-008. User says she did not reply or share her password, and she didn't mention clicking or opening anything, so KB-029 was not triggered. lookup_user: account active, 0 failed logins, not blocked, must_change_password=true (password last set 2026-10-09). She may be a newly set-up account, which would fit a phish aimed at new hires. Her quarantine is empty, so the message got past filtering. Escalated to Tier 2/security (escalation 1) to pull the message, review the sender and headers, block it org-wide, and check for other recipients. Sender address and subject are unknown, so security should get them from her mailbox. If she later says she clicked or entered her password, switch to KB-029 (P1).",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
